OBSERVATIONS (for the lead's backlog -- NOT defects in TC1)
------------------------------------------------------------
OBSERVATION 1 (pre-existing on MASTER, attribution proved, not a FAIL)
  My first matrix ordering (activate the trends tab, THEN click #theme-toggle)
  produced 2 deviating states -- janaug.dark.default and june.dark.default at
  rendered height 300 px instead of 576. I proved this is master-native, not
  TC1's doing, by running the same sequence against the master build:
    MASTER 563bf12 : load+tabclick(light) rectH=1678 -> theme->dark rectH=300
                     -> expand rectH=300 -> collapse rectH=300
    BRANCH 8fba686 : load+tabclick(light) rectH=576  -> theme->dark rectH=300
                     -> expand rectH=1678 -> collapse rectH=576
  Mechanism: activating the tab runs Plotly's resize, which CLEARS
  layout.height; the themechange handler's Plotly.restyle then re-lays-out at
  Plotly's 300 px default. Master never recovers (it only re-renders on a
  re-fetch); the branch recovers on the very next table interaction. Without the
  tab click, master keeps layout.height=1678 across the toggle. TC1 is strictly
  better here, so this is a pre-existing #chart-trends/tab-resize defect for the
  backlog, not a TC1 regression.

OBSERVATION 2 (test strength, V3-pattern follow-up candidate)
  The marker fix that ruling TC-2026-09-08b ordered has NO unit-level guard. Its
  only evidence is my rendered probe, which does not outlive this verdict:
  reverting renderTrendsChart() to inherit fetch-time markers leaves all 18
  node tests green (renderTrendsChart is not exercised by the vm harness --
  document.getElementById returns null there and window.Plotly is absent).
  The brief for attempt 2 asked only for the two height tests, so this is not a
  miss against the criteria. Suggested follow-up: a jsdom/vm case that drives
  renderTrendsChart() with a stubbed renderChart and a flipped `dark` class and
  asserts the marker handed to renderChart is the CURRENT theme's.

OBSERVATION 3 (a11y lane, restated from attempt 1)
  The dark-mode marker colours are SVG fills; axe cannot see them, so
  checker-a11y's scan will not independently confirm item 3. The contrast
  evidence above is mine.
